K5K7M"><header><h1>Pair Sum - Unsorted</h1></header><div class=3D"style_pro=
blemDescription__T4cPe"><p>Given an array of integers, return the indexes o=
f <strong>any two numbers that add up to a target</strong>. The order of th=
e indexes in the result doesn't matter. If no pair is found, return an empt=
y array.</p><h4 id=3D"example">Example:</h4><pre><code class=3D"hljs langua=
ge-python">Input: nums =3D [-<span class=3D"hljs-number">1</span>, <span cl=
ass=3D"hljs-number">3</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number">4</span>, <span c=
lass=3D"hljs-number">2</span>], target =3D <span class=3D"hljs-number">3</s=
pan>
Output: [<span class=3D"hljs-number">0</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number">=
2</span>]
</code></pre><p>Explanation: <code>nums[0] + nums[2] =3D -1 + 4 =3D 3</code=
></p><h4 id=3D"constraints">Constraints:</h4><ul>
<li>The same index cannot be used twice in the result.</li>
</ul></div>
<h2 id=3D"intuition">Intuition</h2>
<p>A brute force approach is to iterate through every possible pair in the =
array to see if their sum is equal to the target. This is the same as the b=
rute force solution described in the <em>Pair Sum - Sorted</em> problem, wh=
ich has a time complexity of <span class=3D"katex"><span class=3D"katex-mat=
hml"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><m=
i>O</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo s=
tretchy=3D"false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">O=
(n^2)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=3D"katex-html" aria=
-hidden=3D"true"><span class=3D"base"><span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height=
: 1.0641em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class=3D"mord mathnormal=
" style=3D"margin-right: 0.02778em;">O</span><span class=3D"mopen">(</span>=
<span class=3D"mord"><span class=3D"mord mathnormal">n</span><span class=3D=
"msupsub"><span class=3D"vlist-t"><span class=3D"vlist-r"><span class=3D"vl=
ist" style=3D"height: 0.8141em;"><span style=3D"top: -3.063em; margin-right=
: 0.05em;"><span class=3D"pstrut" style=3D"height: 2.7em;"></span><span cla=
ss=3D"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class=3D"mord mtight">2</span>=
</span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=3D"mclose">)</=
span></span></span></span>, where <span class=3D"katex"><span class=3D"kate=
x-mathml"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mr=
ow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">n</annotatio=
n></semantics></math></span><span class=3D"katex-html" aria-hidden=3D"true"=
><span class=3D"base"><span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height: 0.4306em;"></s=
pan><span class=3D"mord mathnormal">n</span></span></span></span> is the le=
ngth of the array. We could also sort the array and then perform the two-po=
inter algorithm used in <em>Pair Sum - Sorted</em>, which would take <span =
class=3D"katex"><span class=3D"katex-mathml"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.o=
rg/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">(</m=
o><mi>n</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">(</mo><mi>=
n</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">)</mo><mo stretchy=3D"false">)</mo></mrow><ann=
otation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">O(nlog(n))</annotation></semantics><=
/math></span><span class=3D"katex-html" aria-hidden=3D"true"><span class=3D=
"base"><span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;=
"></span><span class=3D"mord mathnormal" style=3D"margin-right: 0.02778em;"=
>O</span><span class=3D"mopen">(</span><span class=3D"mord mathnormal">n</s=
pan><span class=3D"mord mathnormal" style=3D"margin-right: 0.01968em;">l</s=
pan><span class=3D"mord mathnormal">o</span><span class=3D"mord mathnormal"=
 style=3D"margin-right: 0.03588em;">g</span><span class=3D"mopen">(</span><=
span class=3D"mord mathnormal">n</span><span class=3D"mclose">))</span></sp=
an></span></span> time due to sorting. Let=E2=80=99s see if we can find an =
even faster solution.</p>
<p><strong>Complement</strong><br>
We're asked to find a pair (<code>x</code>, <code>y</code>) such that <code=
>x + y =3D=3D target</code>. In this equation, there are two unknowns: <cod=
e>x</code> and <code>y</code>. An important observation is that if we know =
one of these numbers, we can easily calculate what the other number should =
be.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>For each number x in nums, we need to find another number <code>y</code>=
 such that <code>x + y =3D target</code>, or in other words, <code>y =3D ta=
rget - x</code>. We can call this number the complement of <code>x</code>.<=
/p>
</blockquote>
<p>Keep in mind, we need to return the indexes of the pair of numbers, not =
the pair itself. So, we=E2=80=99ll need a way to find a number's complement=
 as well as its index.</p>
<p>One way we could do this is to loop through the array to find each numbe=
r=E2=80=99s complement and corresponding index. But this takes <span class=
=3D"katex"><span class=3D"katex-mathml"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/19=
98/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">(</mo><ms=
up><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=3D"false">)</mo></mrow><annotati=
on encoding=3D"application/x-tex">O(n^2)</annotation></semantics></math></s=
pan><span class=3D"katex-html" aria-hidden=3D"true"><span class=3D"base"><s=
pan class=3D"strut" style=3D"height: 1.0641em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></=
span><span class=3D"mord mathnormal" style=3D"margin-right: 0.02778em;">O</=
span><span class=3D"mopen">(</span><span class=3D"mord"><span class=3D"mord=
 mathnormal">n</span><span class=3D"msupsub"><span class=3D"vlist-t"><span =
class=3D"vlist-r"><span class=3D"vlist" style=3D"height: 0.8141em;"><span s=
tyle=3D"top: -3.063em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class=3D"pstrut" style=
=3D"height: 2.7em;"></span><span class=3D"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight">=
<span class=3D"mord mtight">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></sp=
an></span><span class=3D"mclose">)</span></span></span></span> time since w=
e=E2=80=99d need to do a linear traversal to search for each number=E2=80=
=99s complement. Instead, we=E2=80=99d like an efficient way to determine t=
he index of any number in the array without needing to search the array. Is=
 there a data structure that can help with this?</p>
<p><strong>Hash map</strong><br>
A hash map works great because we can store and look up values in <span cla=
ss=3D"katex"><span class=3D"katex-mathml"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/=
1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">(</mo><=
mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=3D"false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"applic=
ation/x-tex">O(1)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=3D"kate=
x-html" aria-hidden=3D"true"><span class=3D"base"><span class=3D"strut" sty=
le=3D"height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class=3D"mord mat=
hnormal" style=3D"margin-right: 0.02778em;">O</span><span class=3D"mopen">(=
</span><span class=3D"mord">1</span><span class=3D"mclose">)</span></span><=
/span></span> time. Each number and its index can be stored in the hash map=
 as key-value pairs:</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a transformation of a numerical array into =
a hashmap.  The input is an array `[-1, 3, 4, 2]` with indices `[0, 1, 2, 3=
]` displayed beneath each element. A rightward arrow indicates the transfor=
mation process. The output is a hashmap labeled 'hashmap' which is represen=
ted as a table with two columns: 'num' and 'index'. The 'num' column lists =
the numbers from the input array: -1, 3, 4, and 2. The corresponding 'index=
' column shows the original index of each number in the input array: 0, 1, =
2, and 3 respectively.  The diagram illustrates how the original array's el=
ements and their indices are reorganized into a key-value pair structure wi=
